      "summary": "The adapter’s case-scoped reads and audit writes show no other concrete security defect in the diff. Connection-string validation can accept a SqlClient username alias and permit selection of a different managed identity.",
      "findingList": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "backend/AirlineDemo.Api/Persistence/SqlCaseStateStore.cs",
          "line": 258,
          "message": "OWASP A02:2025: the forbidden-key list omits the SqlClient \"user\" alias for User ID. Reject that alias so a configured user-assigned identity cannot bypass the required no-username check.",
          "confidence": 0.9,
          "category": "security",
          "evidence": {
            "codePathTraced": "The validator rejects only the normalized keys in ForbiddenKeys, then passes the original connection string to UseSqlServer. Microsoft.Data.SqlClient treats \"user\" as an alias for its UserID connection-string property.",
            "failureScenario": "If ConnectionStrings:CaseState contains Authentication=Active Directory Managed Identity;User=<client-id>, registration accepts it. SqlClient can use that value to select a user-assigned managed identity, so the app may connect as an identity other than the intended default app identity."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
